A||||9||Carrolles||Digital volt meter for existing dash cluster
Z||000000||Carrolles||04-22-99||12:47 PM||||Cliff,<br>I want to keep my 85 SE 2m6 4spd as original looking as possible.  I would like to have a volt meter so I can monitor alternator performance.  Would it be possible to put a digital volt meter readout in place of the "SHIFT" light in the dash?  I don't know of anyone who actually pays attention to the shift light anyway.<br>This would retain the original look of the dash cluster.<br>Thanks<br>Carroll
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||04-22-99||01:08 PM||||If your only concern is the operation of the alternator, and you want your interior to be as original looking as possible, why don't you go for the original center gauge-cluster instead (voltmeter and oilpressure)?<p>That would save you a lot of trouble...
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-22-99||10:32 PM||||Hi,<br>The center gauge cluster was not offered until 1986.  I'd like to keep my 85 as original as possible.<br>Thanks<br>Carroll
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||04-23-99||01:52 AM||||Ah ok, I had the year wrong. I thought you were talking about an 86 Fiero (so much for speed reading...)!<p>Anyway, building a digital volt meter is very simple. There are several schematics floating around on the net. I'll see if I can find one.
Z||000004||Carrolles||04-23-99||07:08 AM||||Cliff,<br>That would be great.<br>Thanks<br>Carroll
Z||000005||Carrolles||05-12-99||11:19 PM||||Cliff,<br>By any chance have you found anything for the volt meter in place of the shift light?<br>Thanks,<br>Carroll
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||05-13-99||02:01 AM||||Sorry for the long awaited answer, but check this out:<p> <A HREF="http://www.rpi.edu/~hicksm/elec2.html#voltmeter" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rpi.edu/~hicksm/elec2.html#voltmeter</A> <p>It's a very simple design for measuring voltages up to 40V. If you want, I can take a look at the datasheet for the CA3162E and tell you exactly the value of the resistors to use to make it a bit more accurate when using it in a car. You need very small 7-segment LEDs too, unless you are willing to give up the last digit, giving you a precision of 1V instead of .1V. Let me know.
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||05-13-99||02:03 AM||||Ok, I overlooked the part on that page where he explains how to calculate the value of the resistors yourself...  :)
Z||000008||Carrolles||05-13-99||07:27 AM||||Thanks Cliff.<p>Carroll
Z||000009||theogre||05-13-99||07:39 AM||||if you dig around for multiplex LED displays you can find some prettty small ones. some of them have lenses that make them look bigger than they are. The multiplex display will also save you some wiring time.<p>You might want a bit of input filter before the voltage regulator. a 500uF 25v electrolytic cap should do. Car power is pretty noisy. (The cap will keep the display lit for a few seconds aftedr the key goes off. if it's too long use a smaller cap.)<p>Tantalum caps should work for the 2 that he has notes for. those are faily ruged, and stable over a fairly wide temp range.
